.TH flocate-showdiff 1 "Nov 2025" flocate

.SH NAME
flocate-showdiff \- compare flocate databases or history logs

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flocate-showdiff
.RI [ --history ]
.I FLOCATE_DB
.br
.B flocate-showdiff
.I OLD_DB NEW_DB
.br
.B flocate-showdiff
.RI --live " ROOT " FLOCATE_DB

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flocate-showdiff
inspects the metadata that
.BR updatedb (8)
stores inside a flocate database. It can replay the internal history log,
diff two database snapshots, or compare a database against a live filesystem
root (recomputing metadata and optional hashes).
.PP
Flocate (and thus \fBflocate-showdiff\fR) is a maintained fork of plocate.
The flocate feature set, including this forensic diff tool, has been extended
by Robert Tulke <rt@debian.sh> since November 2025.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --history
Print the recorded add/remove/modify events stored alongside
.I FLOCATE_DB .
The log is ordered chronologically. Each
.B updatedb
run is introduced by a line of the form
.BR "=== run " "DATE" " ===" ;
a run that had no usable previous database is marked as baseline and carries
no per-file events.

.TP
.I OLD_DB NEW_DB
When invoked with two database paths,
.B flocate-showdiff
compares their metadata and prints one line per change (ADDED/REMOVED/MODIFIED)
including the old and/or new attributes.

.TP
.BI --live " ROOT"
Diff a database against the live filesystem rooted at
.IR ROOT .
The tree below
.I ROOT
is walked once, starting at the directory the database was built for (the
.B \-U
root of
.BR updatedb ,
appended to
.IR ROOT ).
Every entry is stat(2)'d, entries missing from the database are reported as
ADDED and database entries missing on disk as REMOVED. Hashes are recomputed
for the files the database stores a digest for (files above the HASH_MAX_SIZE
of the
.B updatedb
run have none and are compared by their other attributes only) and for new
files up to
.BR --hash-max-size .
PRUNEFS, PRUNENAMES and PRUNEPATHS from the configuration block stored in the
database are honoured, pseudo filesystems are never hashed; bind mounts are not
detected in this mode. Use
.I ROOT
= / to check the running system, or the mount point of an image or backup.

.TP
.B --compare-atime
In snapshot and live mode, treat a differing access time alone as a
modification. By default atime is ignored (it changes on every read under
.BR relatime ),
but it is still printed and listed in the
.B changed:
line of MODIFIED events.

.TP
.BI --hash-max-size " SIZE"
In live mode, do not hash files that are missing from the database and larger
than
.I SIZE
(a byte count with an optional K, M, G or T suffix, powers of 1024). Files the
database knows are never affected: they are hashed exactly when the database
stores a digest for them. The default is no limit; pass the HASH_MAX_SIZE the
database was built with to keep a check from reading new large files in full.

.TP
.B --json
Print one JSON object per line instead of the human-readable format. Each
event has the keys
.BR kind ", " path ", " time ", " old ", " new " and " changed ;
run markers have
.BR kind " = " RUN ", " time " and " baseline .
Timestamps are strings of the form
.IR seconds.nanoseconds .
Paths are emitted as byte strings with control characters escaped.

.TP
.B --help
Show a short usage summary.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non-zero on errors (corrupt database, I/O failure,
or command-line usage errors).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I @dbfile@
Default flocate database (as consumed by the history mode).

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR flocate (1),
.BR updatedb (8),
.BR flocate-build (8)

